=part-category p2376
short-description: 3D printable rotary bit

Any 3D-printable part that is intended to be used as
a bit on a drill or impact driver

=part-category p2377
short-description: 3D printable rotary bit with 1/2-inch hex shank

Subset of p2376 that use a 1/2" male hexagon as the drive.

=part p2235
short-description: 90-degree cone with 1/2-inch hex shank
member-of: p2376
member-of: p2377
stl-file: p2235.stl	urn:bitprint:PQ4L7HLSY4LV2S7CLPLEFJNO2LZE3FB5.J35KCHPHFFUS7QQXMXU24G2NX3K75O44YJS2H3I
openscad-rendering-ref: http://picture-files.nuke24.net/uri-res/raw/urn:bitprint:3OEJ2WDOY6INJ56CCNIQ7WXFREJHCHRD.GJCTYQBP7MGSYATV5Y26TTPLSXMEKYLG2FPTZDA/p2235.png

The idea is to superglue some sandpaper or grit onto the cone
and then use it in a drill, using a 1/2 hex socket as an adapter,
to clean out counterbored holes, and stuff.

If it works, I could do other shapes for different-sized
counterbores to be cleaned-out, e.g. my 21mm ones with a 5/16" hole in the moddle.

Obviously there are limitations with 3D-printed parts, which might just up and melt,
but I have wanted to try this for a while.

Findings, via 2025-12-08T19:15-KM:
- Actually a bit small for a 1/2" socket, but fits well in a 12mm one
  (makes sense if 12.7mm - 2*0.3mm = 12.1mm, though I normally expect
  things to be a little fatter)
- It does melt itself in the process of being used, but at the same time,
  it melts down the other pieces, smoothing them both.

